{
 "id": "smithy_village_01",
 "description": "A village blacksmith's forge, inside: rough stone walls with heavy timber posts, a wide open doorway in the south wall with daylight falling in, a flagstone floor, a smoke-darkened beamed plank ceiling. A stone forge hearth with an iron hood and chimney against the north wall; bellows on a trestle at its tuyere (west side); an anvil on an oak stump a step in front of the fire with a quench tub beside it; a heavy workbench with a leg vise against the east wall; a tool rack on the west wall; a charcoal heap, fire tools, bar stock, a grindstone and a water barrel; a lantern hanging from a beam.",
 "style": "Early medieval northern European village smithy (about 1000 AD): grey-brown rubble-coursed stone, dark sooty timber, worn flagstones with ash and charcoal dust, everything smoke-stained, no later-period objects. Light from the doorway (low afternoon sun) and the forge fire, a lantern near the bench, a little smoke haze. North = the hearth wall (the main view), south = the doorway.",
